+---
+id: dl-001-rental-module-deterministic-ids-no-added-tooling-midnight-utc-validation
+type: decision-log
+title: "Rental module: deterministic ids, no added tooling, midnight-UTC validation"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Building `createRentalService()` (addEquipment/quote/book) required a few choices not spelled out in
+the request: how to generate booking ids, how to validate the "whole days, midnight UTC" period rule,
+and how to run TypeScript tests given the project has no runtime or dev dependency today.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Booking ids**: an incrementing per-service-instance counter (`booking-1`, `booking-2`, ...), not a
+  random id or `Date.now()`-based one. The `determinism` directive rules out wall-clock and randomness
+  in the module; a counter is also simpler to assert on in tests.
+- **Period validation**: parse `start`/`end` with `Date.parse` and require each to be an exact multiple
+  of 24h since the epoch (midnight UTC), then require `end > start`. Because UTC has no DST, any pair of
+  midnight-UTC instants is automatically a whole number of days apart, so no separate "whole number of
+  days" check is needed beyond `end > start`.
+- **Tooling**: no TypeScript/build dependency was added. Node 22 (the version available here) strips
+  types natively, so `src/index.ts` and `test/rental-service.test.ts` run directly under
+  `node --test`, matching the "no runtime dependency" description in `README.md`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Booking ids are only unique within one service instance (in-memory, per the request); this is fine
+  for the current in-memory scope but would need revisiting if the service were ever persisted or
+  shared across processes.
+- The native-TypeScript approach ties local test execution to a modern Node version; there is no `tsc`
+  type-check step in this repo yet, so type errors would only surface as the (looser) runtime stripping
+  Node performs, not full strict-mode checking, until a type-check step is added.
+- Cancellation (rule 5) is intentionally not implemented; it will need "now" at cancellation time, which
+  will have to be threaded in explicitly (e.g. as a parameter) to keep honoring the determinism
+  directive.
